<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio Vivaldi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1678">1678</a>–<a href="/years/1741">1741</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio Vivaldi was a Venetian composer, violinist and priest, one of the leading composers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. For much of his career he taught and wrote music for the girls' orchestra of the Ospedale della Pietà in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. He composed hundreds of concertos, including The Four Seasons, as well as operas and sacred music, and his concerto form influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-vivaldi.jpg" alt="Antonio Vivaldi" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vivaldi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antonio Lucio Vivaldi was born on 4 March 1678 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, the son of Giovanni Battista Vivaldi and Camilla Calicchio. He suffered from tightness of the chest, interpreted as a form of asthma, which kept him from playing wind instruments. His father, a barber before he became a professional violinist, taught him the violin. In 1693 he began studying for the priesthood and was ordained in 1703, and he was soon nicknamed il Prete Rosso, the Red Priest, after the colour of his hair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In September 1703 Vivaldi became maestro di violino at the Ospedale della Pietà, an orphanage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> that gave the most talented girls a musical education and a place in its orchestra and choir. He composed most of his major works there over the next thirty years, including concertos, cantatas and more than sixty sacred works. His first opera, Ottone in villa, was performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> in 1713, and he then became impresario of the Teatro San Angelo in Venice. His oratorio Juditha triumphans, composed in 1716 to celebrate the Republic of Venice's victory over the Turks, was sung entirely by the girls of the Pietà.</p><p>In 1717 or 1718 he was offered the post of Maestro di Cappella at the court of Prince Philip of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> in Mantua, and he spent three years there. The Four Seasons, four violin concertos giving musical expression to the seasons, was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1725 as the first four of the twelve concertos of Il cimento dell'armonia e dell'inventione. In 1728 he met the Emperor Charles VI at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, who gave him the title of knight, a gold medal and an invitation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Vivaldi composed more than 500 concertos, over 50 operas and a large body of sacred choral music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vivaldi went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, probably to stage operas, but Charles VI died soon after his arrival and left him without patronage or steady income. He became impoverished and died during the night of 27 to 28 July 1741, aged 63, of an internal infection. He was buried in a simple grave in the Bürgerspital-Gottesacker cemetery.</p><p>After almost two centuries of decline, his reputation revived in the early 20th century. In 1926 researchers found fifteen bound volumes of his music in a monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, containing 300 concertos, 19 operas and over 100 vocal-instrumental works. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> was deeply influenced by his concertos and arias, and transcribed six of them for solo keyboard and three for organ.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonio Vivaldi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…centres of Europe: its school of painting included Giovanni Bellini, Giorgione, Titian, Tintoretto and Paolo Veronese, Andrea Palladio built churches and villas that were copied for centuries, and composers from Claudio Monteverdi to <b>Antonio Vivaldi</b> worked in the city, which opened the first public opera house in 1637.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1718" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antonio Vivaldi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1718">What was here in 1718? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
